#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
genesi-ai-image — local image generation and editing for Genesi AI Mode.

The AI Mode Monitor's Image page is a front end over this tool, the same split
as genesi-ai-turbo / genesi-find: everything that touches models, the disk or
the GPU lives here, where it can be run and tested from a terminal, and the
window only draws what this prints. With --json every subcommand prints one
JSON object per line on stdout; human text goes to stderr.

    genesi-ai-image status   [--json]
    genesi-ai-image catalog  [--json]
    genesi-ai-image pull     MODEL [--json]
    genesi-ai-image remove   MODEL [--json]
    genesi-ai-image generate --model MODEL --prompt TEXT [--ref IMAGE]
                             [--width W --height H] [--steps N] [--seed S]
                             [--out PATH] [--keep-llm] [--json]
    genesi-ai-image upscale  --in IMAGE [--out PATH] [--json]
    genesi-ai-image gallery  [--limit N] [--json]

The engine is stable-diffusion.cpp (package genesi-sd-cpp, `genesi-sd-cli`),
the same ggml family as the llama.cpp behind Turbo. Weights are never shipped:
the user downloads a model from the catalog when they want one, exactly like a
chat model.

── The catalog ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Only models whose licence lets anyone use the output for anything (Apache-2.0
weights, BSD upscaler), and only files that download without a Hugging Face
login. Each file carries its size and SHA-256, so a download that was cut, or
a file that is not the one we tested against, is caught before sd-cli is asked
to load it -- a truncated GGUF does not fail cleanly, it fails as a confusing
tensor error minutes into a load.

Components are shared by NAME across models: a file two models need is
downloaded once, and removing one model never deletes a file the other still
uses.
"""
